hen taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}6{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2a}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{2x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{2x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/61a2aafc6dadee9e392e098f77839fdd99f3bb5d30c62cf06fef1be08c4b2682.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}6{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{2a}) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317069 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317070 13194139534880 true] [17592186317069 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317071 13194139534880 true] [17592186317069 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534880 true] [17592186317069 1041 "field-1" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317069 1104 #edb/ref 17592186317070 13194139534880 true] [17592186317069 1110 #uuid "47c2fc38-cc18-597f-9df5-5b66fb0ac0a2" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317070 1034 "3e^{-2}" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317070 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534880 true] [17592186317070 1111 #uuid "f6748e9a-bfc9-580a-97b9-b0ca4ee46133" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317071 1034 "6e^{-2}" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317071 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534880 true] [17592186317071 1111 #uuid "f36a0474-bf39-5f62-b1be-5f7138e8a915" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317072 1017 #edb/ref 17592186317073 13194139534880 true] [17592186317072 1089 "q-351320" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317072 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534880 true] [17592186317072 1100 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317072 1113 #uuid "0aeedd54-c10a-53bd-b367-88f50ec08852" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317072 1145 "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{3\\cdot {4}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[3\\cdot {4}^{x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(3-3\\cdot {4}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }3\\cdot {4}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={4}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{4}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{4}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/fd4020291b348d130fc34969d72ff683aab5b6fb95ea5f7d32b90e5245d56d9c.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }3\\cdot {4}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317073 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317074 13194139534880 true] [17592186317073 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534880 true] [17592186317073 1041 "field-1" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317073 1104 #edb/ref 17592186317074 13194139534880 true] [17592186317073 1110 #uuid "fa485675-6e54-5354-a5be-95b88c6790a9" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317074 1034 "\\frac{3}{\\ln(4)}" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317074 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534880 true] [17592186317074 1111 #uuid "0945e89d-40a1-5bbd-947b-87ccb466c405" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317075 1017 #edb/ref 17592186317076 13194139534880 true] [17592186317075 1089 "q-351416" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317075 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534880 true] [17592186317075 1100 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317075 1113 #uuid "8db43b84-59e1-5d8b-9409-aff2fd00efef" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317075 1145 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{3x}}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{3x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(8-{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/c2af905a46cea7b7512a727a5e53a2a178e4902f7114f86439365684e6685efa.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317076 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317077 13194139534880 true] [17592186317076 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534880 true] [17592186317076 1041 "field-1" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317076 1104 #edb/ref 17592186317077 13194139534880 true] [17592186317076 1110 #uuid "b096aed0-3798-522b-84a3-5f4f8d7e125f" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317077 1034 "\\frac{4}{3\\ln(2)}" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317077 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534880 true] [17592186317077 1111 #uuid "16d182f3-9d75-54c3-b417-f852e1ef78eb" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317078 1017 #edb/ref 17592186317079 13194139534880 true] [17592186317078 1089 "q-351529" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317078 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534880 true] [17592186317078 1100 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317078 1113 #uuid "808f3d8c-e897-5a7c-8a7c-2cad7570d3cd" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317078 1145 "First, we substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to {0}^{-}$ and $u\\to 0$ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\frac{1}{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{e}^{5u}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{5u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{5u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{e}^{5u}}{5}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{e}^{5a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{5a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{5x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{5x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{5x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042659/assets/2a24352ca6421b3aa61f3f29e3c19373cca011feafce263775a2f692f67ad36b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{5a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317079 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317080 13194139534880 true] [17592186317079 1000 #edb/ref 17592186317081 13194139534880 true] [17592186317079 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534880 true] [17592186317079 1041 "field-1" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317079 1104 #edb/ref 17592186317080 13194139534880 true] [17592186317079 1110 #uuid "c525b007-0a7c-5605-bc48-934795e9ca16" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317080 1034 "\\frac{1}{5}" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317080 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534880 true] [17592186317080 1111 #uuid "264ec97b-82ef-5a8e-b277-97d6e2cf1063" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317081 1034 "-\\frac{1}{5}" 13194139534880 true] [17592186317081 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534880 true] [17592186317081 1111 #uuid "1a89b222-3e93-5590-890c-fa1e2418fd5d" 13194139534880 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-100378" 17592186317